Boardings decline slightly across every rail line and the bus aggregate. This is a real, non-obvious mechanical effect: the trips being trimmed here are, by construction, the region's *longest* commutes — precisely the ones most likely to have used transit in the first place — so some riders shift to shorter drives, walks, or bikes instead. FrontRunner sees the largest absolute rail decline, consistent with it serving the longest-distance commutes.

---

## Free Time Added Back: VHT per Household {#free-time-added-back}

This metric deliberately uses **vehicle-hours of travel (VHT)**, not VHD. VHD (used in the [congestion section](#congestion-vehicle-hours-of-delay) above) only captures the delay component of travel time; VHT is the full realized travel time — free-flow time plus delay — so it also picks up the time households save simply because their trips are now *shorter*, not only because those trips hit less congestion.

